  "interpretation": {
    "confirmed": "The #375 runtime DataReader used KEEP_LAST depth 25 (max_samples 5000; max_samples_per_instance 400). In a 2.434 s interval, all 50 target DATA sequences were processed successfully by StatefulReader, but only sequences 279-303 reached successful DataReader history updates; unread_count peaked at 25.",
    "ab_comparison": "With only the run-local pose reader QoS depth changed to 100, #377's runtime get_qos hook reported KEEP_LAST depth 100 and all 50 sequences 500-549 reached successful DataReader history updates. The history update count increased from 25 to 50 for this comparable 50-sample gap.",
    "limit": "The #377 gap remains 2.410 s with no successful camera-pose take inside it; the first missing RTPS sequences are not returned by the EGO receive API until a later same-sequence copy arrives. This localizes the stall to receive delivery before Fast DDS reader processing, with ordered sequence notification holding later samples. The exact reason the first packets do not reach the receive API remains unproven; the socket counters cannot attribute drops by sequence.",
    "evidence_strength": "The QoS depth and per-sequence history updates are directly observed. The packet-header/receive-API/Fast DDS stage alignment directly shows early captured copies absent from EGO recvfrom/recvmsg records and later copies followed by reader processing. Fast DDS source explains how sequence holes hold notification order. Socket receive-queue pressure is supported as a likely cause, not proven for individual sequences."
  },
