    "The selected take gap is not a full trace acceptance window; the full wait-event sequence check failed in both #375 and #377.",
    "The socket drops counter is cumulative for all traffic on the participant socket and does not map to the target topic or RTPS sequence.",
    "The single A/B establishes a history retention effect for one comparable 50-sample gap, not a universal rate or an accepted production QoS.",
    "Loopback packet capture occurs before application receive; the exact loss/delay for an individual sequence is inferred from the absence of the first copy in EGO recvfrom/recvmsg metadata and the later same-sequence copy. Participant socket drop and queue counters are aggregate, so they do not prove per-sequence kernel drops."
  ],
  "decision": "Keep canonical EGO pose QoS and freshness gates unchanged. Depth 25 explains the 25-sample retention boundary, while RTPS sequence holes and late receive-API delivery explain the selected multi-second wait gaps more directly. Investigate why the first captured datagrams do not reach EGO recvfrom/recvmsg; do not promote depth 100 or relax the 0.4 s freshness gate.",
  "next_step": "Correlate the missing first-copy sequences against per-socket receive-queue/drop instrumentation and Fast DDS reliability control traffic in a repeatable low-noise run. Keep host buffer/kernel parameters and canonical QoS unchanged until sequence-level attribution is available; retain the 0.4 s freshness gate.",
